I. What Is Economics? - - Economics is the study of how people, companies, and governments make choices when resources are limited.
It focuses on relationships among:
•	Buyers
•	Sellers
•	Companies (firms)
•	Governments
Because resources are scarce, these groups must decide what to produce, how to produce, and for whom to produce.
Three Main Divisions of Economics
1.	Microeconomics
	Studies interactions between buyers and sellers.
2.	Macroeconomics
o	Studies the whole economy: companies, governments, inflation, unemployment, GDP.
3.	World Economics (International Economics)
	Studies relationships between governments and governments.
I. The 10 Principles of Microeconomics
1. People Face Trade-offs
A trade-off is when you must choose A or B because you cannot have both.
2. Opportunity Cost
Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative that you give up.
Opportunity cost is something you say byebye to so you can get something else
You always sacrifice something to get something else.
3.   at the Margin
Rational people
Are the people who think with logic
Logic is reasons why you do something (not because you like it)
People who think logically and make decisions by comparing benefits and costs.
Margin (Marginal thinking)
“Margin” = thinking about the next unit.
Rational individuals make decisions by evaluating small changes, not extreme changes.
 	4 . People Respond to Incentives
An incentive is something that motivates a person to act.
5. Trade Can Make Everyone Better Off
Trade allows people, companies, and countries to specialize in what they do best.
“Better off” = wealthier
“Wealthy” = having enough resources to live (food, shelter, money)
6. Markets Are Usually a Good Way to Organize Economic Activity
A market economy allows buyers and sellers to decide what to produce and what to buy.
Prices act as signals:
•	High price → produce more 
•	Low price → produce less


